        "edits": [
          "const ENCODINGS = {\n  br: \".br\",\n  gzip: \".gz\",\n  zstd: \".zst\",\n} as const;\nconst ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS: (keyof typeof ENCODINGS)[] = [\n  \"br\",\n  \"zstd\",\n  \"gzip\",\n];"
        ],
        "finalMessage": "I fixed issue 1 in `index.ts:143`. I couldn't re-run the linter because the folder has no installed dependencies, so the result hasn't been checked by a re-lint.\n\n**Why I didn't just swap the two lines:** the key order in `ENCODINGS` did real work. `ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS` was built with `Object.keys(ENCODINGS)`, and the serving loop (around line 243) uses that order to pick which precompressed file to serve: Brotli (`.br`) first, then zstd (`.zst`), then gzip (`.gz`). Sorting the keys alone would have made the server try gzip before zstd.\n\n**What I changed:**\n- `ENCODINGS` is now in alphabetical order (`br`, `gzip`, `zstd`), which is what the `sort-keys` rule wants.\n- `ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS` is now a written-out list, `[\"br\", \"zstd\", \"gzip\"]`, with the same type as before. So the preference order stays the same, and it no longer depends on the order of the keys in the object.\n\nThe rule only checks object keys, not lists, so it shouldn't fire again.",
